---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const contact = 'macnwen@gmail.com';
const updated = 'August 2026';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What Mac & Wen collects when you sign in, why, and how to have it deleted."
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 mb-10">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <div class="prose prose-stone dark:prose-invert max-w-none
      prose-headings:font-bold prose-headings:text-stone-900 dark:prose-headings:text-stone-100
      prose-a:text-emerald-700 dark:prose-a:text-emerald-400 prose-a:no-underline hover:prose-a:underline">

      <p>
        Mac &amp; Wen is a personal travel blog. You can read all of it without
        signing in, and if you do, we collect nothing about you — there are no
        analytics, no advertising and no tracking cookies anywhere on the site.
      </p>

      <p>
        Signing in with Google is optional, and only needed to manage your own
        packing lists. This page explains what that stores. For anything below,
        write to <a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
      <p>
        Mac &amp; Wen runs this site and decides how the data described here is
        used — the "controller", in the words of the GDPR. Contact:{' '}
        <a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What is stored when you sign in</h2>
      <p>Google tells us, and we keep:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>your name and email address</li>
        <li>a link to your Google profile picture</li>
        <li>the identifier Google uses for your account</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Signing in also creates a record containing:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>the IP address and browser the sign-in came from</li>
        <li>a session token, which is what keeps you signed in</li>
        <li>the times your account and session were created and last updated</li>
        <li>
          the access tokens Google issues, so the sign-in can be completed and
          renewed without asking you again
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We never see or store your Google password. Signing in happens on
        Google's own pages.
      </p>

      <h2>Why it is stored</h2>
      <p>
        To sign you in, keep you signed in, greet you by name, and attach your
        packing lists to your account. That is all of it — the data is not
        profiled, analysed, sold, or used to send you anything. There is no
        mailing list.
      </p>
      <p>
        The lawful basis is that you asked for an account and this data is what
        providing it requires. If you would rather not, simply don't sign in;
        the blog works exactly the same either way.
      </p>

      <h2>Who else can see it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Google</strong> — because you chose to sign in with them.
          Their handling of your Google account is covered by{' '}
          <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
            Google's privacy policy</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloudflare</strong> — hosts the site and the database on our
          behalf, and processes it only for that purpose.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Nobody else. The data is stored in Cloudflare's Western Europe region
        and is not transferred elsewhere by us.
      </p>

      <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
      <p>
        Your account details are kept until you delete your account. Sessions
        expire 30 days after they are created, and are removed when you sign
        out, when an expired one is next presented, or when the account is
        deleted.
      </p>

      <h2>Deleting your account</h2>
      <p>
        Go to <a href="/account">your account</a> and choose "Delete account".
        This immediately and permanently removes your details, your sessions and
        the tokens from Google. It cannot be undone, and nothing is kept behind.
      </p>
      <p>
        For security you need to have signed in within the past day to do this.
        If you are asked to, sign out and back in, then try again.
      </p>
      <p>
        Deleting your account here does not change anything on Google's side.
        You can review which apps have access in your{' '}
        <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
          Google account settings</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        You can ask for a copy of your data, ask for it to be corrected or
        erased, object to it being used, or ask for it in a portable form. Email{' '}
        <a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a> and you will get an answer
        within a month. Deletion you can do yourself, immediately, on the
        account page.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you think your data is being handled wrongly, you can complain to
        your national data protection authority.
      </p>

      <h2>Cookies</h2>
      <p>
        One cookie, set only after you sign in, holding the session token that
        keeps you signed in. It is removed when you sign out. There are no
        analytics or advertising cookies, which is why this site has no cookie
        banner.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If what we collect changes, this page changes with it and the date at
        the top is updated.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
